  "answer": "Current web results retrieved in October 2026 report that the boiling point of water on the summit of Mount Everest (altitude 8,848 m) is between approximately 68°C and 72°C (154°F to 161.5°F) [W1], [W2], [W3], [W4]:\n\n* Some educational sources cite the boiling point at the summit as approximately 68°C (154°F) [W1], [W2].\n* Engineering and physical calculation references state it is approximately 72°C (161.5°F), corresponding to an atmospheric pressure of about 34% of sea level [W3], [W4].\n\nThis is lower than the standard sea-level boiling point of 100°C (212°F) due to the reduced atmospheric pressure at high altitude [W1], [W2], [W4]."
